# Test coverage — non-negotiable

> **Directive: whenever you change a Rust file in this repository, that
> file must have 100% test coverage before the change is considered
> complete.**

This is not advisory. If you touched a file, you are responsible for
proving every code path in it is exercised by tests. The point is corrosion
resistance: one bad parse breaks a chain run that takes hours to redo, and
a wrong UTXO undo entry corrupts live reorg handling in ways that only
show up on a real mainnet reorg.

## How to satisfy it for a file you changed

1. Run the file's existing tests: `cargo test --release <module name>`
2. For any uncovered branch, add a unit test in the same file (`mod tests`)
   or an integration test under `tests/` if the path crosses crate
   boundaries (we already do this for `utxo_reorg` and `parquet_roundtrip`).
3. Every public function you added or changed needs at least one test that
   exercises the happy path and one that exercises a failure mode —
   e.g. `parse_tx` needs a test for a truncated/script-invalid block.
4. Corner-case code paths that exist to mirror consensus or endianness
   quirks (e.g. wire-vs-display hash hex, varint size classes) need
   *assertions on both directions*, not just the happy path.

## Patterns already in this repo that you should follow

- **Roundtrip tests** for every encode/decode pair
  (`UtxoValue::encode/decode`, `BlockUndo::encode/decode`).
- **Truncation/bounds tests** for every parser entry point
  (`BlockUndo::decode` rejects truncated input).
- **Synthetic full-flow tests** via a fake-UTXO store entry
  (`tests/utxo_reorg.rs` constructs tiny in-memory blocks, runs
  `apply_block` then `disconnect_block`, asserts the UTXO set equals the
  genesis fixture).
- **Round-trip through real I/O** for anything persisted
  (`tests/parquet_roundtrip.rs` reads the genesis Parquet back with
  `parquet-arrow` and checks the field values).

## If coverage is impractical

You may only leave a path untested if it is provably unreachable in the
module's domain (e.g. `match` arms guarded by the type system). When you do
this, leave a comment on the line explaining WHY and reference the compile-
time guarantee (e.g. "`unreachable!()` because Foo::Bar is the only
remaining variant"). Do not use `#[allow(unreachable_code)]` to get out of
writing a test.

## Do NOT count these as coverage

- Tests that only call a function and assert its return without verifying
  the state it mutated or the file it wrote.
- Tests that pass "because nothing crashed".
- Coverage measured only by line-count but never asserting a corner case.
